   "y": 18,
   "text": "EASTGATE CLOCK. Built 1899 for Queen Victoria's Diamond Jubilee. The second most photographed clock in Britain, after Big Ben."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "CHESTER CATHEDRAL. Eleven hundred years of worship, one hundred of scaffolding."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "ROMAN GARDENS. Deva Victrix, founded in the AD 70s. The Romans came for the lead, stayed for the drainage."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "NEWGATE. The wall's newest gate. It was built in 1938, so, quite old. By Chester standards, newborn."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "CHESTER STATION. Trains for Wales leave from the platform where the rain does."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "ROMAN AMPHITHEATRE. The biggest in Britain. Seated 8,000 for gladiators. Now seats nobody and hosts the occasional picnic."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 41,
   "text": "THE ROODEE. England's oldest racecourse, racing since 1539. The only sprint here is the ice-cream queue."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 43,
   "text": "THE GROVES. River cruises, ice cream and a swan with an attitude. Do not argue."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "OLD DEE BRIDGE. Medieval, narrow, still carrying traffic. The queue is also medieval."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 5,
   "text": "CHESTER. Roman walls, medieval Rows, Victorian clocks. Come in. Mind the history. It bites."
  },
  {
   "x": 2,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "WEST: Wales, the Dee Valley and a surprising number of sheep."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 2,
   "text": "NORTH: the Wirral Way. And the sea. Remember the sea."
  },
  {
   "x": 62,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "Road to Northwich closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Charles Kingsley, author of The Water-Babies, was Canon of Chester and founded the Chester Society of Natural Science in 1871.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": 0.475,
   "plaque": "chester_charles_kingsley",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Charles Kingsley"
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "chester_name",
   "title": "Name: Chester",
   "text": "Chester comes from the Latin 'castra', a fort, by way of Old English 'ceaster'. Plenty of English towns got a slice of the same word: Lancaster, Manchester, Leicester. In Welsh the city is simply Caer, 'the fort', as if there were only ever one worth mentioning."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "chester_king_charles_tower",
   "title": "King Charles' Tower",
   "text": "In September 1645, during the Civil War, King Charles I is said to have stood on a tower of the city walls to watch his army fight at Rowton Moor, just outside Chester. It went badly. The tower has been called King Charles' Tower ever since: a poor day out for him, a good name for it."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 15,
   "plaque": "chester_mystery_plays",
   "title": "The Mystery Plays",
   "text": "In the Middle Ages Chester's trade guilds put on the Mystery Plays, Bible stories acted on wagons wheeled round the streets, each guild taking a scene. The Waterleaders, who carried water from the Dee, did Noah's Flood. Stopped under Elizabeth I, the plays were revived in the twentieth century."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "chester_st_werburgh",
   "title": "St Werburgh's Geese",
   "text": "St Werburgh, a Mercian princess and abbess, is the saint the abbey here was built for, and her shrine still stands inside. Legend says she ordered a flock of wild geese to stop stealing the crops, and they obeyed. When a servant cooked one, she brought it back to life. Geese have been smug ever since."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 52,
   "text": "SOUTH: the old road to Shrewsbury, the Severn and the Shropshire hills. Mind the pronunciation."
  }
 ],
